Task 1: Pick and Place Object:

In this task, the Franka manipulator was utilized to pick up and place a target cube.
The robot was spawned at the origin (0.0, 0.0, 0.0), and the target cube (a DynamicCuboid) was placed at (0.4, 0.0, 0.02) with a custom light mass and small scale to prevent physics engine clipping.
The RMPFlowController was used to calculate the necessary joint velocities and kinematics to reach the object safely with collision-free trajectories.
The ArticulationAction class was imported to explicitly send float values to the parallel gripper joints (0.04 for open and 0.0 for fully clamped) independently of the arm movement.
A while loop was used to continuously step the physics simulation frame-by-frame, and a frame-counting state machine (using if/elif logic) controlled the exact timing of the sequence: hovering, lowering, grasping, lifting, moving to the drop zone, and releasing.

Task 2: Object Following:

The same robot, the Franka manipulator, was spawned for this environment.
A target DynamicCuboid was spawned and made selectable in the GUI, allowing it to be manually dragged across the environment using the viewport's Translate Gizmo.
The cube.get_world_pose() function was used inside the main physics loop to extract the live X, Y, and Z coordinates of the target cube on every single frame.
A Z-axis offset of 0.20 meters was added to the extracted coordinates to ensure the end-effector hovered securely above the cube rather than crashing into its center.
The dynamically updated, offset coordinates were continuously fed into the RMPFlowController.forward() method, driving the robotic arm to recalculate its trajectory in real-time and actively track the object's movements.
